  "query_preview": "..Current satellite and upper-air data show that a 500\nmb low is moving inland into central California. This system has\nbegun advecting moisture into northern Arizona. Precipitable Water\nvalues, a measure of atmospheric moisture, range from 0.50-0.85\ninches this morning. For comparison, values ranged from 0.25-0.50\ninches yesterday morning. Surface dew points have also increased \nand are generally 5-15 degrees above what was observed yesterday. \nThat said, even with the increase in moisture, model soundings \nindicate thunderstorm development will be hindered by a stable \nlayer around 550mb. The best chances for precipitation (20-30%) \nare forecast across Grand Canyon Country later this afternoon and \novernight, with only a 10-20% chance over higher terrain \nelsewhere. Only light rainfall is expected. Otherwise, expect \nbreezy southerly winds and near-normal daytime temperatures. \n\nOn Tuesday, the low lifts to the northeast, drawing moisture \nprimarily into eastern Arizona. The current forecast calls for a \n40-60% chance for showers and thunderstorms across Navajo and \nApache counties, with decreasing chances farther west. In \naddition, southerly wind speeds are forecast to increase, with \ngusts in the 20-30 mph range. There is only a marginal risk of \nflash flooding over eastern Arizona, as the band of heaviest \nprecipitation is expected to occur farther eastward into New \nMexico. \n\nOn Wednesday, interaction between a high centered over Texas and \na cut-off low off the west coast of Northern Baja turns the flow\nmore southerly resulting in further increases in atmospheric \nmoisture and cooler daytime temperatures. In addition, \nprecipitation should be enhanced by a disturbance moving northward\nout of Mexico. As a result, chances for precipitation and a \nmarginal risk of flash flooding spread farther west to include \nportions of Yavapai County. \n\nAn active weather pattern is expected to remain in place through\nthe remainder of the workweek. On Thursday, the cut-off low is \nforecast to move northeastward in response to a deepening West \nCoast trough. This will likely lead to the most widespread shower \nand thunderstorm activity of the week, with chances for showers \nand thunderstorms continuing into Friday. High uncertainty exists\nregarding the chances for precipitation over the weekend due to \nuncertain interactions among another West Coast trough, and \nHurricane Polo/Tropical Storm Odalys currently located in the \neastern tropical Pacific.",
